# Rules for a smooth, robust cursor/command worker (PyQt frontend)

## 1 — Event-driven first, polling only when necessary

* Prefer **signals/slots** and callbacks over frequent timers. Timers hide state and cause UI jitter.
* Use timers only for *optional* periodic UI refreshes (status badges) — keep interval ≥ 1s and back off on errors.
* Where possible push logic to the backend and let the frontend subscribe to the response queue (no polling).
* If you must poll (e.g., RabbitMQ management endpoint), make polling adaptive: increase interval on repeated failures, reset on success.

## 2 — Keep UI thread free: network & IO in worker threads

* Never block the Qt main thread. All RabbitMQ, Redis, heavy JSON parsing, and shell result handling must run in `QThread` or worker process.
* Use a **worker object** moved to `QThread`, and emit Qt signals for results/errors back to `QWidget` classes.
* Example pattern (simplified):

```python
# worker.py
class RabbitWorker(QObject):
    response_received = pyqtSignal(dict)
    error = pyqtSignal(str)

    @pyqtSlot()
    def run(self):
        # long-running rabbitmq consume loop - emit response_received when a message arrives
        ...
```

* Use `Qt.QueuedConnection` to ensure thread-safe signal delivery.

## 3 — Decouple modules; single responsibility

* Split responsibilities into modules: `UI`, `CommandWorker` (RPC client), `SessionManagerUI`, `ApiClient` (REST helper), `Config`, `Logger`, `StateModel`.
* Each module should expose a small, well-documented public API and hide internals.
* Avoid business logic inside widgets — widgets should call into a controller/service.

## 4 — Use robust threading & concurrency primitives

* Use `QMutex` or Python threading primitives when sharing small state across threads. Prefer message passing (signals) over shared mutable state.
* For per-session command queues use async queues inside worker thread (FIFO) or promise/chain pattern — ensure commands for a session are executed sequentially.

## 5 — API / message contract: strict schemas & validation

* Define JSON schemas for commands and responses. Validate on send and on receive. Reject/make safe any malformed payload.
* Use version field in messages (`v: "1.0"`) so breaking changes are explicit.

## 6 — Message ordering & idempotency

* Attach `commandId` (UUID) and `sessionId` to every message. Display commandId in developer terminal for traceability.
* Backend must make idempotent operations where possible or at least include `sequence` number per-session so frontend can reorder or deduplicate.

## 7 — Reliable delivery & acknowledgements

* Use durable queues and persistent messages for commands that must not be lost.
* Acknowledge messages only after they are forwarded to UI/state model. Support requeueing on failure when appropriate.

## 8 — Reconnect & backoff strategy

* Automatic reconnect with **exponential backoff + jitter**.
* Emit connection status signals (`connected`, `disconnected`, `connecting`) so UI can react.
* When disconnected: show offline UI state, disable inputs that would fail, allow queuing locally and automatic replay on reconnect if safe.

## 9 — Timeouts & cancellation

* Enforce timeouts on long-running commands (configurable, default 15s).
* Allow user to cancel a pending command: send a `cancel` message to backend and mark command cancelled in UI. Backend should support cancellation tokens.

## 10 — Session state & path isolation

* Keep a local session model in UI: `sessionId`, `currentPath`, `previousPath`, `status`. Update only from authoritative responses.
* Do not assume `cd` succeeded until backend confirms and returns `currentPath`.

## 11 — Caching awareness in UI

* Show clear cache indicators (e.g., small badge `cached`) when response had `cached: true`.
* If frontend caches UI-side, honor TTL and invalidate on navigation or stateful commands.

## 12 — Graceful shutdown & cleanup

* On app quit, disable new commands, flush pending actions, close RabbitMQ consumer cleanly, then quit threads.
* Persist last-known session states/log positions to disk if it helps recovery.

## 13 — Clear error handling & user feedback

* Distinguish error types: network, auth, validation, execution, internal server error. Display helpful messages — include `commandId` for developer mode.
* Always log raw errors to a file but show friendly messages in GUI.

## 14 — Security & secrets

* Never store RabbitMQ/Redis passwords in plaintext. Use environment variables or an encrypted store.
* Use TLS for RabbitMQ where possible. Validate server certificates.
* Sanitize user-provided `execute_raw` commands (or restrict this to developer mode).

## 15 — Minimal, predictable UI state transitions

* Each command lifecycle: `queued → sending → running → finished/error`. Represent these states in UI (spinner, progress, etc.).
* Avoid UI flicker by animating transitions subtly and batching UI updates for many responses.

## 16 — Logging & telemetry

* Structured logs (JSON lines) per session and system logs. Include timestamps, `commandId`, `sessionId`, `workerId`.
* Expose a log viewer in Server tab that streams logs (tail) without blocking.

## 17 — Health & diagnostics

* Expose simple diagnostics: RabbitMQ/Redis connection, last heartbeat, queue depth (prefetch), last error.
* Allow the frontend to request `GET /health` and a `GET /queues` snapshot.

## 18 — Testability: unit/integration

* Unit-test CommandWorker with a mocked RabbitMQ client.
* Provide integration tests that run a local RabbitMQ + fake backend to verify end-to-end message flow.
* Add UI tests (pytest-qt) for key flows: connect/disconnect, send command, receive response.

## 19 — Backpressure & QoS

* Respect backend prefetch and QoS: do not flood backend with concurrent “heavy” commands.
* Implement a local rate-limit per-session and a global concurrency limit.

## 20 — Keep APIs stable and backward-compatible

* When changing message schemas, support old versions for a migration window. Emit deprecation warnings.

## 21 — UX rules specific to developer terminal vs user controls

* Developer terminal: full `execute_raw`, show stdout/stderr and timings, allow large buffers, show raw JSON toggle.
* User Control tab: expose safe, high-level commands, friendly labels and confirmations for destructive actions.

## 22 — Resource limits & safety

* Impose max buffer sizes (e.g., 10MB) for command outputs shown in UI and truncate with download link for full logs.
* Warn user before executing heavy commands (e.g., `ps aux` in huge hosts).

## 23 — Observability: metrics & alerts

* Track metrics: `commands/sec`, `avg execution time`, `cache hit rate`, `error rate`, `queue depth`.
* Show simple graphs in Server tab and send alerts for repeated failures.

## 24 — Configuration & defaults

* Provide sane defaults in `config` but allow overrides via env vars or settings dialog.
* Keep a `config.sample` checked into repo.

## 25 — Developer ergonomics

* Provide a “Replay” or “Resend” function in developer terminal for previously sent commands.
* Make command payload editable before resend.

